/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · Modern History (batch y) */
WB.L("spanish-armada", "history", "beginner", "The Spanish Armada, 1588", "The most powerful empire on Earth sent a giant fleet to conquer England. Fireships, bad maps and brutal storms decided what happened next.",
  [
    ["A quarrel between two monarchs", "The Armada grew out of a long religious and political feud between Catholic Spain and Protestant England.",
      "Picture two rulers who had once been in-laws. Philip II of Spain had been married to Mary I of England, Elizabeth I's older half-sister. After Mary died, Elizabeth steered England firmly towards Protestantism, while Philip saw himself as the champion of Catholic Europe. The quarrel deepened for decades. English privateers such as Francis Drake raided Spanish treasure ships and colonies, and Elizabeth sent soldiers to help Dutch Protestants rebelling against Spanish rule. The breaking point came in 1587, when Elizabeth allowed the execution of Mary, Queen of Scots, the Catholic cousin many hoped would replace her. To Philip, invading England now looked like a matter of faith, security and honour all at once. He began assembling the largest fleet his kingdom had ever sent north."],
    ["The grand plan", "Spain's fleet was meant to escort an invasion army across the Channel, not to win England alone.",
      "The Armada that sailed in 1588 numbered around 130 ships, carrying sailors, soldiers, priests, horses and enormous quantities of supplies. Its commander was the Duke of Medina Sidonia, a wealthy nobleman with little experience at sea, who had reportedly tried to decline the job. The fleet was not supposed to conquer England by itself. Its task was to sail up the English Channel, link up with the Duke of Parma's veteran army waiting in the Spanish Netherlands, and protect the barges carrying those troops across to Kent. The flaw was obvious in hindsight. There was no deep water port where the big ships and Parma's barges could safely meet, and messages between the two dukes travelled slowly. Coordinating them under enemy fire would prove almost impossible."],
    ["Cat and mouse in the Channel", "For a week the English harassed the Armada but could not break its tight formation.",
      "In late July 1588 the Spanish ships were sighted off Cornwall, and warning beacons flared along the coast. The Armada moved up the Channel in a disciplined crescent formation, with the strongest warships protecting the slower supply vessels at the centre. The English fleet, led by Lord Howard of Effingham with Drake as one of his senior captains, had faster, nimbler ships and longer range guns. Spanish tactics relied on closing in, grappling and boarding, turning a sea fight into an infantry battle. The English refused to let them get close. Over several days the two fleets exchanged long range fire off Plymouth, Portland and the Isle of Wight. Both sides burned through gunpowder and shot, but the Armada stayed largely intact as it anchored off Calais."],
    ["Fireships at Calais", "A night attack with burning ships broke the Armada's formation and opened the way to a decisive battle.",
      "Anchored off Calais, the Armada was vulnerable. Parma's army was not ready, and the Spanish had nowhere safe to wait. On the night of 7 to 8 August, by the modern calendar, the English packed eight old ships with tar, pitch and anything that would burn, set them alight and let wind and tide carry them towards the anchored fleet. Fireships terrified wooden navies, and many Spanish captains cut their anchor cables and scattered into the darkness. By morning the famous crescent was gone. The English attacked off Gravelines, close to the Flemish coast, and this time they came in closer. Several Spanish ships were wrecked or captured, many were badly damaged, and the wind pushed the rest away from Parma's waiting army."],
    ["The long way home", "Storms and shipwrecks on the voyage around Scotland and Ireland cost Spain far more than English guns did.",
      "With the wind blowing from the south and the English fleet behind them, the Spanish could not turn back down the Channel. Medina Sidonia chose to sail north around Scotland and down the west coast of Ireland, a huge detour through unfamiliar, stormy waters. The ships were battered, short of water and food, and their charts of those coasts were poor. Autumn gales drove many onto the rocks of Ireland and Scotland. Thousands of men drowned, and many who reached shore were killed or captured. Historians think roughly a third or more of the fleet never made it back to Spain, and a far larger share of the men died. Most of those losses came from weather, disease and hunger rather than from battle."],
    ["Elizabeth at Tilbury", "The Armada crisis helped turn Elizabeth I into a national legend.",
      "While the danger was still real, Elizabeth travelled to Tilbury, on the Thames, to review troops gathered to resist an invasion. Accounts written afterwards record her saying that she had the body of a weak and feeble woman, but the heart and stomach of a king, and of a king of England too. Historians debate exactly what she said, because the best known version was written down years later, but the image stuck. After the victory, English propaganda presented the Armada's defeat as a sign of God's favour, describing the storms as a Protestant wind. Medals celebrated the event and portraits showed Elizabeth with the wrecked fleet behind her. For centuries the story was told as proof that a small island could defy a superpower."],
    ["Myth and reality", "The Armada did not end Spanish power, but it helped shape how England saw itself.",
      "Popular history often treats 1588 as the moment England became a great sea power and Spain began to decline. The reality is messier. The war dragged on until 1604. An English counter attack on Spain and Portugal in 1589 failed badly, and Philip sent further armadas in the 1590s, which were scattered by storms before they could land. Spain remained the dominant power in Europe for decades and rebuilt its navy quickly. Yet the Armada's failure mattered. It kept Protestant England independent, encouraged the Dutch revolt, and gave English sailors confidence for later ventures across the oceans. Perhaps its biggest legacy was a national story about plucky defiance against invasion, one that would be retold in 1805, 1940 and beyond."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which event in 1587 pushed Philip II to finally launch the invasion?", ["The death of Mary I", "The execution of Mary, Queen of Scots", "The founding of Virginia", "Drake's voyage around the world"], 1, "Elizabeth allowed Mary, Queen of Scots, a Catholic claimant to the throne, to be executed in 1587."],
    [1, "What was the Armada's main job?", ["To blockade London", "To capture Ireland", "To escort the Duke of Parma's army across to England", "To seize the English treasure fleet"], 2, "The fleet was meant to link up with Parma's army in the Netherlands and protect its crossing."],
    [3, "What broke up the Armada's formation off Calais?", ["A sudden fog", "A night attack with fireships", "A mutiny on the flagship", "French cannon on the shore"], 1, "Burning ships sent in at night made Spanish captains cut their anchors and scatter."],
    [4, "What caused most of the Armada's losses?", ["Storms, shipwrecks, hunger and disease on the way home", "English boarding parties", "Dutch privateers", "A plague outbreak in Spain"], 0, "The voyage around Scotland and Ireland in autumn gales was far deadlier than the fighting."],
    [6, "What happened after 1588?", ["Spain surrendered immediately", "England invaded Spain successfully in 1589", "Spain never built another fleet", "The war continued until 1604 and Spain sent more armadas"], 3, "The war dragged on, and later Spanish fleets were also scattered by storms."]
  ]);

WB.L("great-fire-of-london", "history", "beginner", "The Great Fire of London", "In September 1666 a small fire in a bakery grew into a firestorm that swallowed a medieval city. What rose from the ashes still shapes London today.",
  [
    ["A spark in Pudding Lane", "The fire began in the early hours of 2 September 1666 at a bakery near London Bridge.",
      "Sometime after midnight on Sunday 2 September 1666, a fire started in the bakery of Thomas Farriner on Pudding Lane, a narrow street close to London Bridge. Farriner later insisted he had put out his oven before bed, but the house was soon ablaze and the family escaped across the rooftops. Fires were common in seventeenth century London, so at first few people panicked. The Lord Mayor, Sir Thomas Bludworth, was woken and reportedly decided the blaze was not worth demolishing neighbouring houses for. It was a costly misjudgement. The summer had been long and dry, the timber buildings were tinder, and a strong east wind was blowing. By morning the fire had reached the warehouses along the Thames, packed with oil, tallow, coal and brandy."],
    ["A city built to burn", "Crowded timber houses, narrow lanes and riverside warehouses gave the fire everything it needed.",
      "London in 1666 was still largely a medieval city within its old Roman and medieval walls. Houses were made of timber and plaster, often with overhanging upper storeys called jetties that almost touched across the narrow streets, so flames could leap from one side to the other. Rubbish, hay and firewood were stored everywhere. Firefighting technology was basic: leather buckets, axes, simple hand pumps and long hooks for pulling down buildings. There was no organised fire brigade. The city had also just been through the Great Plague of 1665, which killed perhaps a fifth of its population, so many Londoners were already exhausted and frightened. When the fire reached the riverfront, the warehouses acted like fuel depots, and the blaze became too hot for anyone to approach."],
    ["Four days of fire", "The fire raged from Sunday to Wednesday, driven by wind and checked at last by firebreaks.",
      "For about four days the fire swept west and north across the city. People fled with whatever they could carry, piling belongings into carts and boats, and prices for transport soared. The heat was so intense that St Paul's Cathedral, which was covered in wooden scaffolding for repairs, caught fire; its lead roof melted and reportedly ran down the streets. King Charles II and his brother James, Duke of York, took charge, joining the effort to create firebreaks by pulling down or blowing up houses with gunpowder. On Tuesday night the wind finally eased. Combined with the firebreaks, this allowed the fire to be brought under control by Wednesday and Thursday, though cellars and rubble smouldered for weeks afterwards."],
    ["Pepys and his parmesan", "Eyewitness diaries give us vivid, human details of the disaster.",
      "Much of what we know comes from two famous diarists, Samuel Pepys and John Evelyn. Pepys, a naval administrator, watched from a boat on the Thames and described pigeons hanging around their homes until their wings caught fire. He hurried to warn the king and urged that houses be pulled down. Like many Londoners he also worried about his valuables. He records digging a pit in his garden with a colleague to bury wine and his parmesan cheese, along with official papers. Evelyn described the sky glowing red for miles and the ground so hot it was hard to walk on. These diaries show not only the scale of the fire but also the confusion, fear and everyday practicality of people caught in it."],
    ["Counting the cost", "The fire destroyed most of the old city, though the recorded death toll was surprisingly low.",
      "When the smoke cleared, around 13,000 houses had been destroyed, along with 87 parish churches, St Paul's Cathedral, the Guildhall and many other public buildings. Tens of thousands of people were homeless, camping in the fields north of the city. Only a handful of deaths were officially recorded, but historians think the true number was higher, since the deaths of poor people and anyone consumed by the intense heat may never have been counted. People wanted someone to blame. Foreigners and Catholics were attacked in the streets. A French watchmaker named Robert Hubert confessed to starting the fire and was hanged, even though evidence later suggested he had not even been in London when it began. An inquiry eventually concluded the fire was an accident."],
    ["Rebuilding London", "Grand plans for a new city lost out to practical rebuilding, but with new safety rules.",
      "Within days, architects including Christopher Wren and John Evelyn presented plans for a new London with wide boulevards and open piazzas, rather like a European capital. None was adopted. Property owners wanted their land back quickly, and sorting out who owned what was hard enough already. Instead, the city was rebuilt largely along its old street pattern, but with important changes. New laws required buildings to be made of brick or stone, set limits on heights and widened some streets. Wren designed the new St Paul's Cathedral, with its famous dome, which took several decades to complete, along with dozens of new city churches. A special Fire Court settled disputes between landlords and tenants so rebuilding could get moving."],
    ["The fire's long shadow", "From insurance to a famous column, the Great Fire left lasting marks on modern life.",
      "Near Pudding Lane stands the Monument, a tall stone column designed by Wren and Robert Hooke. It is 202 feet high, roughly 61 metres, and sits about 202 feet from where the bakery stood. The fire also helped launch modern property insurance: in the following years, businesses began offering cover against fire, and some ran their own teams of firefighters to protect insured buildings. A popular story says the fire ended the plague by burning the rats, but historians are doubtful, because the plague was already declining and also faded in areas the fire never touched. What is certain is that the disaster changed how cities thought about fire safety, building materials and planning, lessons that later shaped building codes around the world, including in Australia."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Where did the Great Fire of London start?", ["In St Paul's Cathedral", "At the Tower of London", "In a bakery on Pudding Lane", "In a theatre on the South Bank"], 2, "It began in Thomas Farriner's bakery on Pudding Lane, near London Bridge."],
    [1, "Why did flames spread so easily between houses?", ["The houses were timber, with upper storeys that nearly touched across narrow lanes", "Houses had thatched stone roofs", "The streets were paved with wood", "Gas lamps exploded"], 0, "Overhanging timber jetties let fire leap across the narrow streets."],
    [3, "What did Samuel Pepys famously bury in his garden?", ["Gold coins and jewels", "His parmesan cheese and wine", "The king's letters", "His diary"], 1, "Pepys records burying wine, official papers and his parmesan cheese."],
    [5, "What did new building rules require after the fire?", ["Wooden houses with fire buckets", "Thatched roofs soaked in water", "Houses no taller than one storey", "Buildings made of brick or stone"], 3, "Rebuilding laws required brick or stone construction and set height limits."],
    [6, "What is notable about the Monument's height?", ["It matches its distance from the site of the bakery", "It is the tallest structure in Europe", "It is exactly as tall as St Paul's", "It was built in a single day"], 0, "The column is 202 feet tall and stands about 202 feet from where the fire started."]
  ]);

WB.L("east-india-company", "history", "intermediate", "The East India Company: a business that ruled", "It began as a group of London merchants chasing pepper. Within two centuries it had its own army and ruled millions of people across India.",
  [
    ["Merchants and a royal charter", "The East India Company began in 1600 as a trading venture backed by investors and a royal monopoly.",
      "On the last day of 1600, Elizabeth I granted a charter to a group of London merchants, giving them the sole English right to trade with Asia. Their target was spices, especially pepper, which earned huge profits in Europe. The Company was an early joint stock venture: investors pooled money to fund risky voyages and shared the returns. Its great rival, the Dutch East India Company, founded in 1602, was richer and more aggressive, and soon pushed the English out of the most lucrative spice islands in what is now Indonesia. So the English turned to India instead. There they found something even more valuable than pepper: cotton textiles. Bright, washable Indian calicoes and chintzes became a fashion craze back home."],
    ["Factories and forts", "For over a century the Company was a trader that depended on the favour of Indian rulers.",
      "In its early years the Company was a junior player in a rich, powerful region. Much of India was ruled by the Mughal Empire, one of the wealthiest states in the world. The Company negotiated with emperors and local governors for permission to trade, setting up trading posts called factories. Its main bases grew into Madras, now Chennai, in the south; Bombay, now Mumbai, which came to England as part of Charles II's marriage settlement and was handed to the Company; and Calcutta, now Kolkata, in Bengal. These posts were fortified and guarded by small private forces. As Mughal power weakened in the eighteenth century and regional rulers competed for control, the Company began to use its soldiers and money to play politics as well as trade."],
    ["Plassey: a battle and a betrayal", "In 1757 the Company won control of Bengal more through intrigue than fighting.",
      "Bengal was one of the richest provinces in India, and in 1756 its young ruler, the Nawab Siraj ud-Daulah, captured Calcutta after the Company strengthened its fortifications without permission. A Company officer, Robert Clive, sailed north to take it back. Clive was ambitious and ruthless, and he found allies among Bengal's elite who wanted the Nawab gone, including his army commander Mir Jafar. At the Battle of Plassey in 1757, Clive's much smaller force faced the Nawab's army, but much of that army never fought because its commanders had already agreed to switch sides. Siraj ud-Daulah was later killed and Mir Jafar was installed as a puppet ruler. Huge sums flowed to the Company and to Clive personally, who returned to Britain extremely rich."],
    ["From trader to tax collector", "Winning the right to collect Bengal's taxes turned the Company into a government.",
      "The real turning point came in 1765. After further fighting, the weakened Mughal emperor granted the Company the diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Orissa: the right to collect land revenue across a vast and populous region. Instead of bringing silver from Britain to buy Indian goods, the Company could now use Indian taxes to pay for them, and for its growing army. Its officials were poorly supervised and often grew rich through private trade and bribes, earning the mocking nickname 'nabobs' back in Britain. In 1770 a devastating famine struck Bengal. Millions died, possibly as much as a third of the population in the worst areas, and critics accused the Company of making things worse by keeping up harsh tax collection while people starved."],
    ["Too big to fail", "Britain's government stepped in when the Company's power and debts became a national problem.",
      "By the early 1770s the Company was both mighty and in financial trouble, and Parliament grew alarmed that a private business was running an empire. The Regulating Act of 1773 began bringing it under government oversight, and later laws went further, creating a government board to supervise its political decisions. That same year, the Tea Act tried to help the struggling Company sell surplus tea in the American colonies, which led directly to the Boston Tea Party. In Britain, Warren Hastings, the first Governor General of Bengal, was impeached for corruption and abuse of power in a famous trial that ran for seven years. He was eventually acquitted, but the trial showed how uneasy many Britons felt about the Company's conduct in India."],
    ["An army with a company attached", "By the early 1800s the Company commanded an army larger than Britain's own.",
      "Through the late eighteenth and early nineteenth centuries the Company fought a series of wars against Indian powers, including the Kingdom of Mysore and the Maratha Confederacy. Most of its soldiers were Indian troops known as sepoys, led by British officers. Historians estimate its armies numbered in the hundreds of thousands at their peak, larger than the regular British Army at the time. By the 1850s the Company directly ruled much of the subcontinent and controlled many princely states through treaties. It also grew opium in India and sold it to traders who smuggled it into China, helping to pay for its purchases of Chinese tea. Meanwhile, Parliament stripped away its trading monopolies, leaving it more a government than a business."],
    ["Rebellion and the end", "After the uprising of 1857, the British Crown took over, and the Company faded away.",
      "In 1857 a mutiny by sepoys at Meerut spread into a widespread rebellion across northern India, fuelled by many grievances, from annexations and taxes to fears about religious interference. The fighting was brutal on both sides, and the British response was savage. Afterwards, Parliament decided the Company could no longer be trusted with India. In 1858 the British Crown took direct control, beginning the period known as the Raj, and the Company was formally dissolved in 1874. Its legacy is debated to this day. It left behind cities, railways planned in its final years and words such as 'loot', borrowed from Hindi. It also stands as a warning about what can happen when a profit seeking corporation holds unchecked political and military power."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What did the East India Company first come to Asia for?", ["Gold", "Silk", "Spices, especially pepper", "Tea"], 2, "The Company's founders were chasing the hugely profitable spice trade."],
    [2, "How did Robert Clive win at Plassey in 1757?", ["With a much larger army", "Using new rifles", "By sea bombardment alone", "Because key commanders in the Nawab's army had agreed to switch sides"], 3, "Mir Jafar and others had secretly agreed to betray Siraj ud-Daulah, so much of his army did not fight."],
    [3, "What was the diwani the Company gained in 1765?", ["The right to collect land revenue in Bengal and nearby regions", "A monopoly on Indian cotton", "Ownership of Bombay", "A seat in the Mughal court"], 0, "The diwani let the Company collect taxes, turning it into a governing power."],
    [4, "Which American event was linked to the Company's troubles?", ["The Salem witch trials", "The Boston Tea Party", "The Louisiana Purchase", "The California gold rush"], 1, "The 1773 Tea Act, meant to help the Company sell tea, sparked the Boston Tea Party."],
    [6, "What happened to India after the 1857 rebellion?", ["It became independent", "France took control", "The British Crown took direct control", "The Mughal Empire was restored"], 2, "In 1858 the Crown took over from the Company, beginning the Raj."]
  ]);

WB.L("meiji-japan", "history", "intermediate", "Meiji Japan: the great makeover", "In about forty years, Japan went from a closed realm of samurai and shoguns to an industrial power that defeated a European empire. How did it happen so fast?",
  [
    ["A closed country", "For more than two centuries Japan strictly limited contact with the outside world.",
      "In the early 1600s the Tokugawa family unified Japan after long civil wars and ruled as shoguns, military leaders who held real power while the emperor lived in ceremonial seclusion in Kyoto. Worried about Christian missionaries and European influence, the shoguns restricted foreign contact. Japanese people were generally forbidden to travel abroad, and European trade was limited to the Dutch, confined to a small artificial island called Dejima in Nagasaki harbour. Trade with China and Korea continued under controls. Inside these walls Japan was not frozen. Cities such as Edo, now Tokyo, grew huge, literacy was relatively high and arts like kabuki theatre and woodblock prints flourished. But Japan's rulers had little idea how quickly Western industry and military technology were changing."],
    ["The black ships arrive", "American warships in 1853 forced Japan to open up and exposed the shogunate's weakness.",
      "In July 1853 four American ships steamed into Edo Bay under Commodore Matthew Perry. Two were steam powered, belching black smoke, and the Japanese called them the black ships. Perry carried a letter demanding that Japan open its ports to American ships for supplies and trade, and he made it clear he would return. When he came back in 1854 with a larger squadron, the shogunate signed the Treaty of Kanagawa. Agreements with other Western powers soon followed, including 'unequal treaties' that fixed low tariffs and let foreigners be tried in their own courts rather than Japanese ones. Many Japanese were humiliated. The shogun seemed unable to protect the country, and his critics began rallying around the emperor instead."],
    ["Restoration of the emperor", "In 1868 rebel samurai toppled the shogunate and ruled in the name of a teenage emperor.",
      "Through the 1860s, ambitious samurai from southwestern domains, especially Satsuma and Choshu, plotted against the Tokugawa. Their early slogan was 'revere the emperor, expel the barbarians', but clashes with Western warships taught them that expelling foreigners was impossible. They concluded Japan would have to learn from the West to resist it. In late 1867 and early 1868 the last shogun gave up power, and after a short civil war the new leaders declared the emperor restored to direct rule. The emperor was a teenager named Mutsuhito, and his reign took the name Meiji, usually translated as 'enlightened rule'. In practice a small group of young oligarchs governed in his name. The imperial court moved to Edo, which was renamed Tokyo, meaning 'eastern capital'."],
    ["The end of the samurai", "Japan's new leaders dismantled the old class system, even though most of them were samurai themselves.",
      "The new government moved astonishingly fast. In 1871 it abolished the old feudal domains and replaced them with prefectures run from Tokyo. The rigid class system was dismantled, and samurai lost their hereditary stipends, which were converted into government bonds. A conscript army of ordinary men replaced the samurai's monopoly on fighting, and in 1876 the wearing of swords in public was largely banned. Many samurai adapted, becoming officials, teachers, police officers or businessmen. Others resisted. The biggest uprising was the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877, led by Saigo Takamori, once a hero of the restoration. The new conscript army crushed it, and Saigo died in the final battle. He became a romantic legend, loosely echoed much later in the Hollywood film The Last Samurai."],
    ["Learning from the world", "Japan studied Western nations closely and adopted what it thought worked best.",
      "In 1871 a large government delegation known as the Iwakura Mission set off to visit the United States and Europe. It spent well over a year touring factories, schools, parliaments, shipyards and prisons, taking careful notes. Japan then borrowed selectively: a navy modelled on Britain's, an army influenced first by France and later by Germany, legal codes drawing on French and German law, and a school system with ideas from several countries. The government hired thousands of foreign experts, then gradually replaced them with Japanese graduates. A popular slogan captured the goal: 'rich country, strong army'. Another phrase described the balance many hoped for, Japanese spirit with Western techniques. Clothing, hairstyles and even the calendar changed, as Japan adopted the Western solar calendar from 1873."],
    ["Railways, silk and a constitution", "Industry, education and a new constitution turned Japan into a modern state.",
      "Japan's first railway, between Tokyo and Yokohama, opened in 1872, and lines soon spread across the country. The government built model factories, including a large silk mill at Tomioka, then sold many enterprises cheaply to private businesses, helping create powerful family conglomerates known as zaibatsu, such as Mitsubishi and Mitsui. Silk exports earned the foreign currency Japan needed to buy machinery and warships. Primary schooling became compulsory, and literacy climbed even higher. In 1889 the emperor granted a written constitution, influenced by Prussia's, which created an elected parliament called the Diet. Voting was restricted to a small minority of wealthy men, and the emperor was declared sacred and inviolable, but it made Japan the first Asian nation with a Western style constitution."],
    ["A new power, and a warning", "Victories over China and Russia made Japan a great power, but also set it on the road to empire.",
      "Japan's transformation was tested in war. In 1894 to 1895 it defeated China and took Taiwan as a colony. Then, in 1904 to 1905, it shocked the world by defeating Russia on land and at sea, the first time in modern history that an Asian nation had beaten a European great power in a major war. Western powers soon agreed to end the unequal treaties. When the Meiji emperor died in 1912, Japan was an industrial and colonial power that had annexed Korea in 1910. The Meiji era is often held up as a model of rapid modernisation. But the same drive for national strength, and the army's growing political power, helped set Japan on a path towards militarism, expansion in Asia and, eventually, the Pacific War."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which Europeans could still trade with Tokugawa Japan?", ["The Portuguese", "The British", "The Dutch, on the island of Dejima", "The Spanish"], 2, "Dutch traders were confined to Dejima in Nagasaki harbour."],
    [1, "What did Commodore Perry's 1853 visit demand?", ["That Japan open its ports to American ships", "That Japan convert to Christianity", "That Japan hand over Okinawa", "That the emperor abdicate"], 0, "Perry demanded access to Japanese ports for supplies and trade."],
    [3, "What was the Satsuma Rebellion of 1877?", ["A peasant revolt over rice prices", "A samurai uprising crushed by the new conscript army", "A war against Russia", "A rebellion in Taiwan"], 1, "Saigo Takamori led samurai against the government, and the conscript army defeated them."],
    [5, "What did the 1889 constitution create?", ["Full voting rights for all adults", "A republic", "A military government", "An elected parliament called the Diet"], 3, "It established the Diet, though only a small minority of men could vote."],
    [6, "Why was Japan's victory over Russia in 1904 to 1905 so significant?", ["It ended the samurai class", "It was the first modern defeat of a European great power by an Asian nation in a major war", "It gave Japan control of China", "It began the Meiji Restoration"], 1, "The victory stunned the world and confirmed Japan as a great power."]
  ]);

WB.L("opium-wars", "history", "advanced", "The Opium Wars and China's century of humiliation", "Britain went to war so its merchants could keep selling a drug China had banned. The consequences still shape China's view of the West.",
  [
    ["Britain's tea problem", "Britain's thirst for Chinese tea created a trade imbalance that opium was used to fix.",
      "By the late 1700s the British were drinking enormous amounts of tea, and almost all of it came from China. The trouble was that China wanted very little that Britain made. The Qing government also strictly controlled foreign trade, confining Western merchants to the port of Canton, now Guangzhou, where they dealt through licensed Chinese merchants. So the British paid in silver, and silver flowed steadily out of Britain towards China. The East India Company found a solution in the fields of Bengal, which it controlled. It grew opium there and sold it at auction in Calcutta to private traders. Those traders carried it to the Chinese coast and smuggled it ashore, keeping the Company's own hands technically clean. Silver began flowing the other way."],
    ["A nation in the grip of a drug", "Opium smuggling exploded in the early 1800s, alarming the Qing government.",
      "China had banned opium smoking and imports, but the bans were widely ignored. Smugglers worked with corrupt officials, and by the 1830s tens of thousands of chests were arriving each year. Addiction spread from the wealthy to soldiers, officials and labourers, and opium dens appeared in many cities. For the government, the crisis was both moral and financial. Officials worried about the damage to people and to the army, and about the drain of silver out of the country, which was pushing up the cost of silver and making taxes harder for ordinary farmers to pay. In Beijing there was a fierce debate over whether to legalise and tax the trade or to crush it. The Daoguang Emperor chose to crush it."],
    ["Commissioner Lin takes a stand", "In 1839 Lin Zexu seized and destroyed a vast stockpile of foreign opium.",
      "The emperor sent an incorruptible official, Lin Zexu, to Canton in 1839 with orders to end the trade. Lin arrested Chinese dealers, pressured smokers to give up the drug and then blockaded the foreign merchants in their warehouses until they surrendered their stocks. The British superintendent of trade, Charles Elliot, persuaded the merchants to hand over more than 20,000 chests, promising that the British government would compensate them. Lin had the opium mixed with lime and salt in large trenches at Humen and flushed out to sea, a process that took weeks. He also wrote a letter addressed to Queen Victoria, asking how Britain could allow a poison it banned at home to be sold to China. Historians doubt she ever read it."],
    ["The First Opium War", "Britain sent warships, and modern firepower overwhelmed Qing forces.",
      "News of the seized opium caused uproar among British merchants, who lobbied for war. In Parliament some members, including a young William Gladstone, condemned the idea as unjust and shameful, but the government sent a naval force anyway. The war that began in 1839 and ran until 1842 was lopsided. British warships, including the iron steamship Nemesis, which could navigate shallow rivers, destroyed Chinese war junks and coastal forts. British forces captured key cities and finally threatened Nanjing, the old southern capital. The Qing armies fought bravely in places but were outgunned and badly coordinated. Officials sometimes sent misleading reports to Beijing, so the emperor did not fully grasp how badly things were going until it was too late."],
    ["The Treaty of Nanking", "The peace of 1842 began a series of unequal treaties forced on China.",
      "The Treaty of Nanking, signed in 1842, was the first of what Chinese later called the unequal treaties. China paid a large indemnity, including compensation for the destroyed opium. It handed Hong Kong Island to Britain and opened five ports, including Shanghai, to British trade and residence. Supplementary agreements gave British subjects extraterritoriality, meaning they would be tried under British law rather than Chinese law, and guaranteed Britain any privileges China granted to other nations. The United States and France soon secured similar treaties. Strikingly, the treaty did not mention opium. The drug trade carried on and grew. Foreign concessions in the treaty ports became small enclaves where Chinese authority barely reached, and Shanghai grew into a booming international city."],
    ["The Second Opium War", "A second war, fought by Britain and France, ended with the burning of the emperor's summer palace.",
      "Western powers wanted still more access, including diplomats in Beijing and trade inland. In 1856 Chinese officials boarded the Arrow, a ship registered in Hong Kong, and Britain used the incident to go to war again, this time alongside France. The Second Opium War dragged on until 1860. When Qing officials detained and mistreated a British and French negotiating party, some of whom died, the British envoy Lord Elgin ordered the destruction of the Old Summer Palace outside Beijing, a vast complex of gardens and pavilions. Troops looted its treasures, many of which remain in Western collections, and burned it. The treaties that followed opened more ports, allowed foreign envoys in Beijing, ceded Kowloon to Britain and effectively legalised the opium trade."],
    ["A century of humiliation", "The Opium Wars remain central to how China understands its modern history.",
      "The wars weakened the Qing dynasty at a terrible time. The Taiping Rebellion, which began in 1850, killed perhaps 20 million people or more, and further defeats and concessions followed for decades. In 1898 Britain leased the New Territories next to Kowloon for 99 years, which is why Hong Kong returned to Chinese rule in 1997. In China, the period from 1839 to the founding of the People's Republic in 1949 is often called the century of humiliation, and it features heavily in school textbooks and national messaging. Many in the West know little about the Opium Wars. Understanding them helps explain why China's leaders talk so often about sovereignty, foreign interference and national rejuvenation, and why history remains a sensitive subject in relations today."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Britain begin selling opium to China?", ["To fund the Napoleonic Wars", "To pay for tea without sending so much silver", "Because China requested it", "To weaken Russia"], 1, "Opium sales reversed the flow of silver that buying Chinese tea had created."],
    [2, "What did Commissioner Lin Zexu do with the seized opium?", ["Sold it to Russia", "Burned it in Beijing", "Mixed it with lime and salt and flushed it out to sea", "Stored it as medicine"], 2, "More than 20,000 chests were destroyed in trenches at Humen."],
    [3, "What advantage helped Britain win the First Opium War?", ["Superior naval firepower, including the steamship Nemesis", "A much larger army", "An alliance with Japan", "Support from the Qing emperor's rivals"], 0, "Modern warships, including the iron steamer Nemesis, overwhelmed Chinese defences."],
    [4, "Which territory did China cede to Britain in 1842?", ["Macau", "Taiwan", "The New Territories", "Hong Kong Island"], 3, "The Treaty of Nanking handed Hong Kong Island to Britain."],
    [6, "Why did Hong Kong return to China in 1997?", ["Britain lost a war", "The 99 year lease on the New Territories expired", "A referendum was held", "The Treaty of Nanking set that date"], 1, "The 1898 lease ran out in 1997, and Britain returned the whole colony."]
  ]);

WB.L("thirty-years-war", "history", "advanced", "The Thirty Years' War", "It began with three men thrown from a castle window and became one of the deadliest wars in European history. Its peace treaties helped shape the idea of the modern state.",
  [
    ["Out the window in Prague", "The war began with a dramatic act of defiance in Prague in 1618.",
      "In May 1618, a group of angry Protestant nobles stormed into Prague Castle, seized two Catholic officials representing the Habsburg emperor, along with their secretary, and threw them out of a high window. Remarkably, all three survived the fall. Catholics claimed angels had saved them; Protestants mocked that they had landed in a dung heap. The event, known as the Defenestration of Prague, was a protest against the Habsburgs, who ruled Bohemia and were restricting Protestant rights there. The Bohemian nobles then rejected their Habsburg king and offered the crown to a Protestant prince, Frederick V of the Palatinate. A local revolt was about to grow into a conflict that would drag in most of Europe's major powers for three decades."],
    ["A patchwork empire", "The Holy Roman Empire's complex politics made the conflict hard to contain.",
      "To understand the war, picture central Europe as a patchwork. The Holy Roman Empire covered much of what is now Germany, Austria, the Czech Republic and more, but it was not a unified country. It contained hundreds of states, from large principalities to tiny territories and free cities, each with its own ruler. The emperor, usually a Habsburg, had real but limited authority. Since the Peace of Augsburg in 1555, each ruler could choose whether their territory was Lutheran or Catholic, a principle later summed up as 'whose realm, his religion'. Calvinism, a growing Protestant movement, had not been included in that settlement. Religious tensions, rival dynasties and foreign powers eager to weaken the Habsburgs made the empire a tinderbox waiting for a spark."],
    ["From Bohemia to Denmark", "The Catholic Habsburgs crushed the Bohemian revolt and then pushed north, alarming Protestant Europe.",
      "Frederick V's reign in Bohemia was so brief that he became known as the Winter King. In 1620 imperial and Catholic League forces crushed his army at the Battle of White Mountain, outside Prague. Bohemia was forcibly returned to Catholicism, Protestant nobles were executed or driven out, and their lands were handed to loyal supporters. The war then spread. King Christian IV of Denmark, a Lutheran who also held lands within the empire, intervened in 1625 but was defeated. The emperor's armies were now led partly by Albrecht von Wallenstein, a brilliant and enormously wealthy military entrepreneur who raised his own huge army and paid for it by forcing occupied regions to supply money and food. By the late 1620s the Catholic side seemed close to total victory."],
    ["The Lion of the North", "Sweden's King Gustavus Adolphus transformed the war before dying in battle.",
      "In 1630 a new force landed on the German coast: King Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden, a Lutheran with a highly trained, disciplined army and modern tactics, including mobile field artillery and flexible infantry formations. Nicknamed the Lion of the North, he defeated the imperial army decisively at Breitenfeld in 1631 and swept deep into southern Germany. His campaign was funded partly by Catholic France, which cared more about weakening the Habsburgs than about religion. In 1632 Gustavus was killed at the Battle of Lutzen, though the Swedes won the battle itself. Two years later Wallenstein, suspected by the emperor of secret negotiations and disloyalty, was assassinated by his own officers. The war had lost its most famous commanders but showed no sign of ending."],
    ["Catholic France against Catholic Austria", "When France entered openly in 1635, the war became a struggle for power more than for faith.",
      "In 1635 France, guided by its chief minister Cardinal Richelieu, openly declared war on Spain and soon fought the Austrian Habsburgs too. This was a striking moment: a Catholic kingdom, led by a cardinal of the Catholic Church, fighting alongside Protestant Sweden and German princes against the Catholic Habsburgs. It showed that the war had become mainly about power and the balance of Europe. France feared being encircled by Habsburg lands in Spain, Italy, the Netherlands and Germany. The fighting spread across many fronts and grew increasingly about who would dominate the continent. Meanwhile, armies on all sides lived off the land, marching back and forth across the same German territories, plundering towns and villages again and again."],
    ["Hunger, plague and ruin", "Civilians suffered most, through massacre, famine and disease.",
      "The worst single atrocity was the sack of Magdeburg in 1631, when imperial troops stormed the Protestant city and it burned. Historians estimate that about 20,000 people died, and the city was left almost empty. Yet most deaths in the war came not from battles but from what armies brought with them. Soldiers seized crops and livestock, burned farms and spread diseases such as typhus and plague. Refugees crowded into towns where hunger and sickness followed. Estimates vary, but many historians think the German lands lost roughly a fifth or more of their population, with some regions losing far more. Artists recorded the horror, notably Jacques Callot in his etchings The Miseries of War, and the novel Simplicius Simplicissimus captured the chaos through a wandering soldier's eyes."],
    ["Westphalia and the modern state", "The peace of 1648 redrew Europe's map and helped define how states deal with each other.",
      "Peace talks dragged on for years in two Westphalian towns, Munster and Osnabruck, with delegations from across Europe. The resulting Peace of Westphalia in 1648 recognised the independence of the Dutch Republic from Spain and of the Swiss Confederation, gave territory to France and Sweden, and allowed German princes greater freedom to run their own affairs. Calvinism was finally recognised alongside Lutheranism and Catholicism. Political scientists often describe 'Westphalian sovereignty', the idea that states are equal and should not interfere in each other's internal affairs, as born here. Historians point out that this is partly a later myth, since the treaties themselves were more complicated. Even so, Westphalia marked a shift away from wars fought mainly over religion towards a Europe of competing sovereign states."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was the Defenestration of Prague?", ["The burning of Prague Castle", "Protestant nobles throwing Habsburg officials from a castle window", "The execution of the Bohemian king", "A siege of Prague by Sweden"], 1, "In 1618 Protestant nobles threw two officials and their secretary out of a window, and all three survived."],
    [1, "Which Protestant group had been left out of the Peace of Augsburg in 1555?", ["Lutherans", "Anglicans", "Calvinists", "Hussites"], 2, "Augsburg let rulers choose Lutheranism or Catholicism, but Calvinism was not included."],
    [3, "What happened to Gustavus Adolphus in 1632?", ["He was killed at the Battle of Lutzen", "He became Holy Roman Emperor", "He signed a peace with France", "He was assassinated by his officers"], 0, "He died at Lutzen, although the Swedes won the battle."],
    [4, "Why was France's entry into the war in 1635 so striking?", ["France was neutral and Protestant", "France fought for Spain", "France was led by a Protestant king", "A Catholic kingdom fought against the Catholic Habsburgs"], 3, "Led by Cardinal Richelieu, France fought the Habsburgs to protect its own power."],
    [6, "Which country's independence did the Peace of Westphalia recognise?", ["Belgium", "The Dutch Republic", "Poland", "Portugal"], 1, "The 1648 settlement recognised the Dutch Republic's independence from Spain."]
  ]);

WB.L("kokoda-track", "history", "beginner", "Kokoda: the track that shaped a nation", "In 1942, on a muddy jungle path in Papua, young Australian soldiers fought to stop a Japanese advance on Port Moresby. Kokoda became one of Australia's defining wartime stories.",
  [
    ["The threat from the north", "In 1942 Japan's rapid advance brought the war close to Australia's doorstep.",
      "Early in 1942, Japan seemed unstoppable. Singapore fell in February, and thousands of Australian soldiers became prisoners of war. Days later Darwin was bombed. Papua, on the island of New Guinea, was then an Australian territory, and its capital, Port Moresby, lay just across the Torres Strait from Queensland. Japanese commanders wanted to capture it as a base that could threaten northern Australia and cut its links with the United States. An attempt to reach Port Moresby by sea was turned back after the Battle of the Coral Sea in May 1942. So in July Japanese forces landed on Papua's northern coast, near Buna and Gona, planning to march overland across the rugged Owen Stanley Range and seize the town from behind."],
    ["A track, not a road", "The Kokoda Track was a narrow, steep path through some of the world's toughest terrain.",
      "The Kokoda Track runs for about 96 kilometres between the outskirts of Port Moresby and the village of Kokoda, which had a small airstrip. It is not a road but a footpath, used for generations by local people, climbing and plunging over steep ridges and through dense rainforest. Soldiers described climbing slippery slopes on hands and knees, crossing fast rivers and sleeping in mud as rain fell day after day. It was hot and humid in the valleys and cold at night on the high ridges. Malaria, dysentery and exhaustion struck everyone. Every bullet, tin of food and stretcher had to be carried in on someone's back. Historians often say the terrain itself was as much an enemy as the opposing army."],
    ["The young men of the 39th", "Poorly trained militia troops were the first to face the Japanese advance.",
      "When the Japanese landed, the only Australian troops in their path were a small force of local Papuan police and soldiers, and the 39th Battalion. The 39th was a militia unit made up of young, mostly inexperienced volunteers and conscripts, many still in their teens. Some regular soldiers had mocked militia troops as 'chocos', or chocolate soldiers, who would melt under pressure. On Kokoda they did nothing of the sort. Heavily outnumbered, they fought a series of desperate actions around Kokoda village, losing and briefly retaking it before being forced back. In August, under a new commander, Lieutenant Colonel Ralph Honner, they held on at Isurava until reinforcements arrived. Their stand became one of the proudest stories in Australian military history."],
    ["Isurava and a fighting withdrawal", "Experienced AIF battalions joined the fight, slowing the Japanese at a heavy cost.",
      "In late August, battle hardened soldiers of the Second Australian Imperial Force, veterans of fighting in the Middle East, reached the front. The 2/14th and 2/16th Battalions joined the 39th in a fierce battle at Isurava against a much larger Japanese force. During this fighting, Private Bruce Kingsbury charged forward firing a Bren gun from the hip, breaking up an enemy attack before he was killed by a sniper. He was awarded the Victoria Cross after his death, the first awarded for action on Australian territory. The Australians could not hold Isurava, but they made the Japanese pay for every ridge. Over the following weeks they conducted a fighting withdrawal back along the track towards Port Moresby, exhausted, hungry and sick."],
    ["The carriers of the track", "Papuan carriers moved supplies forward and carried the wounded back, saving countless lives.",
      "The campaign could not have been fought without thousands of Papuan men recruited, some under pressure, to work as carriers. They hauled heavy loads of food and ammunition up the mountains and, most famously, carried wounded Australians on stretchers for days through the jungle, sheltering them at night and treating them with great care. Soldiers wrote with deep gratitude about their kindness. A poem by an Australian sapper, Bert Beros, called them the Fuzzy Wuzzy Angels, and the name stuck for decades, though some now consider it outdated. The carriers were poorly paid and often worked in harsh conditions themselves. In recent years Australia has done more to recognise their contribution, which many veterans believed had never been properly honoured."],
    ["Turning the tide", "By late 1942 the Japanese had been ordered back, and Australians pushed them to the coast.",
      "By mid September the Japanese had reached Ioribaiwa Ridge, within sight of the lights of Port Moresby, according to many accounts. But they were starving and stretched thin at the end of a long supply line. Japan had also suffered setbacks elsewhere, including defeat by Australian forces at Milne Bay, on Papua's eastern tip, and heavy fighting with the Americans at Guadalcanal. The Japanese were ordered to withdraw. Australian troops then pursued them back along the track, fighting hard battles at places such as Eora Creek and Oivi to Gorari, and recaptured Kokoda on 2 November 1942. The campaign ended with bloody battles alongside American troops at Buna, Gona and Sanananda, which lasted into January 1943."],
    ["Kokoda in memory", "Kokoda has become a pilgrimage site and a central part of how Australians remember the war.",
      "More than 600 Australians were killed on the Kokoda Track and over a thousand were wounded, with many more struck down by disease. Japanese losses were much greater, many from starvation and illness. Cameraman Damien Parer filmed the campaign, and his newsreel Kokoda Front Line! won an Academy Award, bringing the jungle war home to cinema audiences. For decades Kokoda received less attention than Gallipoli, but that began to change. In 1992 Prime Minister Paul Keating knelt and kissed the ground at Kokoda, arguing that Australians had fought there to defend their own country. Today thousands of Australians walk the track each year with Papuan guides, a hard trek that ends at memorials honouring both the soldiers and the carriers."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why did Japanese forces want to capture Port Moresby?", ["To use it as a base threatening Australia and its links with the United States", "To reach the gold fields of Papua", "To rescue prisoners of war", "To build a railway to Darwin"], 0, "Port Moresby would have given Japan a base close to northern Australia."],
    [2, "Which Australian unit first fought the Japanese on the track?", ["The 2/14th Battalion", "The 9th Division", "The 39th Battalion, a militia unit", "The Light Horse"], 2, "The young militiamen of the 39th faced the early Japanese advance."],
    [3, "Who was awarded a Victoria Cross for his actions at Isurava?", ["John Simpson Kirkpatrick", "Ralph Honner", "Damien Parer", "Bruce Kingsbury"], 3, "Private Bruce Kingsbury was awarded the VC after his death, the first for action on Australian territory."],
    [4, "What role did Papuan carriers play?", ["They guarded Port Moresby", "They carried supplies forward and wounded soldiers back", "They flew supply planes", "They built the airstrip at Darwin"], 1, "Papuan carriers moved vital supplies and carried the wounded for days through the jungle."],
    [6, "What did Paul Keating do at Kokoda in 1992?", ["Opened a new road", "Knelt and kissed the ground", "Signed a peace treaty", "Unveiled a statue of Kingsbury"], 1, "Keating kissed the ground, highlighting Kokoda's place in Australian memory."]
  ]);

WB.L("chernobyl", "history", "beginner", "Chernobyl: the night a reactor exploded", "In April 1986 a late night safety test went catastrophically wrong in Soviet Ukraine. The disaster spread radiation across Europe and shook the Soviet Union to its core.",
  [
    ["A test in the middle of the night", "The disaster began during a poorly managed safety test at reactor number 4.",
      "The Chernobyl nuclear power plant stood in the north of Soviet Ukraine, near the new town of Pripyat, home to tens of thousands of workers and their families. In the early hours of 26 April 1986, operators at reactor number 4 were running a test. They wanted to check whether the spinning turbines could keep supplying power to cooling pumps for a short time if the electricity supply failed. The test had already been delayed, so a night shift that had not prepared for it carried it out. To run it, operators reduced the reactor's power and disabled several safety systems. The reactor became unstable at low power, and operators withdrew most of the control rods to try to keep it running. It was a deadly combination."],
    ["Why the reactor blew up", "Design flaws in the Soviet RBMK reactor combined with operator errors to cause an explosion.",
      "Chernobyl used a Soviet design known as the RBMK, which had dangerous features that operators were not fully told about. In this design, if cooling water turned to steam, the reactor's power could increase rather than fall, a feedback loop that can run out of control. When the test began, power started to rise. An operator pressed the emergency shutdown button, sending control rods back into the core. But a flaw in the rods meant that, for a few seconds, their tips actually boosted the reaction in parts of the core. Power surged enormously. Steam explosions blew apart the reactor and threw off its heavy lid. The graphite inside caught fire, sending radioactive material high into the sky for days."],
    ["Heroes and casualties", "Firefighters and plant workers fought the blaze, many unaware of the danger they faced.",
      "Firefighters raced to the plant within minutes, fighting fires on the roof and around the reactor building. Few understood how intense the radiation was. Some reportedly picked up chunks of graphite thrown from the core. Two plant workers died on the night of the explosion, and in the following weeks and months around 28 more, mostly firefighters and staff, died from acute radiation sickness. Many were flown to a specialist hospital in Moscow, where doctors could do little. Over the following days, helicopters dropped thousands of tonnes of sand, clay, lead and other materials onto the burning reactor to smother it. Later, miners dug beneath the reactor to install cooling, and huge numbers of workers known as liquidators were brought in for the cleanup."],
    ["Pripyat empties", "Residents of Pripyat were evacuated after about a day and a half and never returned.",
      "On the day after the explosion, life in Pripyat continued almost as normal. Children played outside and a wedding went ahead. Then, roughly 36 hours after the accident, residents were told to pack for a short evacuation and boarded a long line of buses. About 49,000 people left the town, believing they would return within days. They never did. Over the following weeks the evacuation zone was widened to a radius of about 30 kilometres, and more than 100,000 people were eventually moved from the area. Today Pripyat is a ghost town, its apartment blocks crumbling and trees growing through the floors. Its unused amusement park, with a Ferris wheel that was due to open days later, has become a haunting symbol of the disaster."],
    ["Secrecy and a Swedish alarm", "The Soviet government admitted the accident only after radiation was detected in Sweden.",
      "At first the Soviet authorities said almost nothing publicly. The breakthrough came from Sweden. On 28 April, workers arriving at the Forsmark nuclear plant, more than 1,000 kilometres away, set off radiation alarms. Swedish experts checked their own plant, found nothing wrong and traced the radioactive particles to winds blowing from the Soviet Union. Under pressure, Soviet state media issued a short statement that evening acknowledging an accident. Radioactive fallout drifted across Belarus, Ukraine and Russia, and on to much of Europe, with rain bringing it down unevenly. Farmers in places as distant as Wales and Scandinavia faced restrictions on sheep and reindeer for years. The Soviet leader, Mikhail Gorbachev, later suggested that Chernobyl was a turning point that exposed the failings of the Soviet system."],
    ["Counting the cost", "The long term death toll is still debated, but thyroid cancer in children is a clear consequence.",
      "The full human cost of Chernobyl is hard to measure. Its clearest health effect was a sharp rise in thyroid cancer among people who were children at the time, mainly because radioactive iodine got into milk from cows grazing on contaminated grass. Thousands of cases were diagnosed, although with treatment most patients survived. Estimates of eventual cancer deaths vary widely. A major United Nations led review in 2005 estimated that up to about 4,000 deaths might eventually result among the most heavily exposed groups, while some other studies give much higher figures for Europe as a whole. Many evacuees also suffered serious stress, anxiety and upheaval. The economic cost to Ukraine, Belarus and Russia ran into hundreds of billions of dollars over the following decades."],
    ["The zone today", "A giant steel shelter now covers the reactor, and wildlife has returned to the exclusion zone.",
      "Within months of the disaster, workers built a concrete shelter, nicknamed the sarcophagus, over the shattered reactor. It was rushed and began to deteriorate, so an international project built an enormous arched steel structure called the New Safe Confinement, which was slid into place over the old shelter in 2016. The plant's last working reactor was shut down in 2000. With people gone, the exclusion zone has become an unexpected wildlife refuge, home to wolves, elk, deer and a herd of rare Przewalski's horses, though scientists still debate radiation's effects on the animals. In 2022 Russian troops occupied the site for several weeks during the invasion of Ukraine, a reminder that the legacy of Chernobyl is still tied to today's politics."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What were operators doing when the Chernobyl accident began?", ["Refuelling the reactor", "Running a safety test on power supply to cooling pumps", "Shutting the plant down permanently", "Testing a new type of fuel"], 1, "The night shift was testing whether turbines could power cooling pumps during an electricity failure."],
    [1, "What flaw made the emergency shutdown backfire?", ["The tips of the control rods briefly boosted the reaction", "The alarm system was switched off", "The water supply froze", "The reactor had no control rods"], 0, "A design flaw meant the rods' tips increased power for a few seconds as they entered the core."],
    [3, "Roughly how long after the accident was Pripyat evacuated?", ["Two hours", "Two weeks", "About 36 hours", "Six months"], 2, "Around 49,000 residents left Pripyat by bus roughly a day and a half after the explosion."],
    [4, "How did the outside world first learn of the disaster?", ["A Soviet press conference", "A spy satellite photo", "A letter from Gorbachev", "Radiation alarms at a Swedish nuclear plant"], 3, "Workers at Forsmark in Sweden detected radiation and traced it to the Soviet Union."],
    [5, "What was the clearest health effect of Chernobyl?", ["A rise in thyroid cancer among people exposed as children", "An outbreak of plague", "Widespread blindness", "A rise in heart disease across Europe"], 0, "Radioactive iodine in milk led to thousands of thyroid cancer cases among those exposed as children."]
  ]);
